WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:06.800
Alan Turing is someone who spent a lot of time&nbsp;
thinking about the mystery of consciousness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:13.280
and what it means to be alive. His work in&nbsp;
computing also tackled these questions and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.280 --> 00:00:18.880
with his ideas about the Turing machine&nbsp;
and the Turing test he no doubt brushed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:25.280
up against the questions of whether a computer&nbsp;
could be considered to be alive or conscious&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:31.040
and where a classification would leave&nbsp;
the field of artificial intelligence.

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:38.160
As a boy Alan Turing read a book called&nbsp;
Natural Wonders by Edwin Tenney Brewster&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:43.200
and he later told his mum that it&nbsp;
was this book that opened his heart&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:50.080
and mind to science. The book explores lots&nbsp;
of wonderful ideas around the science of life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:56.320
including questioning aliveness and a&nbsp;
chapter on where we do our thinking.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:03.440
While doing research for this video I enjoyed this&nbsp;
book so much that I wanted to read it out to you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.440 --> 00:01:10.800
and so i've started a podcast doing just that. The&nbsp;
first episode about how the chicken gets inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:17.120
the egg is up now for everyone and the rest of&nbsp;
the episodes will be available through my Patreon.

00:01:17.920 --> 00:01:23.200
For now though let's have a look at the&nbsp;
chapter called how much of us is alive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:31.200
The author talks about how much of a tree could&nbsp;
be considered to be alive. The bark is dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:39.120
and the wood is dead between the two is a thin&nbsp;
layer which is alive. The green leaves are alive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:44.880
and the twigs but at least half of&nbsp;
every living tree is already dead.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:53.840
The blood we say is the life and yet the blood&nbsp;
itself is dead. The watery part is just soup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.480 --> 00:02:02.640
water and salt and fat and jelly. The minute&nbsp;
coin-like red blood corpuscles carry the oxygen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:10.240
of the air from the lungs all over the body but&nbsp;
there are similar oxygen carriers likewise dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.240 --> 00:02:17.200
in bottles in the drugstore. Maybe this question&nbsp;
of whether our blood is considered alive or dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.320
could be better left to philosophers but&nbsp;
this is the view that the author holds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:29.040
and he describes us as being made of&nbsp;
living bricks but set in a dead mortar.

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.680
Perhaps it is the incompleteness of this&nbsp;
explanation that inspired Alan Turing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:42.400
to want to explore how to get from a&nbsp;
series of living bricks to intelligence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.280 --> 00:02:50.160
and Turing did have a lifelong passion for&nbsp;
biology. I'll put on screen some pictures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:57.200
from some of his less known work on the topic&nbsp;
of morphogenesis, in trying to understabd&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:03.600
the patterns in living things whether it's in the&nbsp;
fur of animals and the patterns and the colors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:09.520
all the way down to the way the seeds arrange&nbsp;
themselves in a flower head. Turing was trying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:16.400
to understand how this could happen from kind of a&nbsp;
mathematical perspective and these ideas are also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:23.040
particular favorites of mine. I've always been&nbsp;
really fascinated by this interface of biology&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:28.880
and maths and finding patterns in nature so I&nbsp;
really love having a look at some of this work.

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:35.840
Questioning the nature of aliveness is&nbsp;
also something that influenced the way that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:42.800
Alan Turing approached childhood grief. An&nbsp;
important part of the story of Alan Turing's life&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:49.520
is that of his childhood best friend and&nbsp;
possibly first love Christopher Morcom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.160 --> 00:03:55.760
Alan and Christopher would spend a lot of time&nbsp;
together at the Sherborne school library where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:02.080
they would discuss their ideas in mathematics&nbsp;
and science. Christopher was a year older than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:10.000
Alan and also a very gifted mathematician. He had&nbsp;
won a scholarship to study at Cambridge University&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:17.120
but he died in 1930 at age 18 before&nbsp;
he was ever able to take up his place.

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:23.920
He died from bovine tuberculosis which&nbsp;
came from drinking infected cow's milk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.920 --> 00:04:26.400
and the loss was really difficult for Turing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:33.440
he stayed close with Christopher's mother and&nbsp;
wrote her a letter all about his thoughts on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:40.720
spirit in which he says that the body provides&nbsp;
something for the spirit to look after and use.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:47.920
It sounds like this was his way of wrapping his&nbsp;
head around the loss and also comforting her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:52.080
Turing also resolved to work&nbsp;
extra hard going forward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.080 --> 00:04:55.840
to make up for the great&nbsp;
potential that had been lost.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:03.280
Christopher's mum created a science prize at the&nbsp;
school to help remember Christopher by and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:11.360
very first winner of that prize was Alan Turing,&nbsp;
and as part of his prize he got to select a book.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:16.560
The one he chose was called&nbsp;
Mathematical Recreations and Essays.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:20.160
The last chapter of this book&nbsp;
was about cryptographs and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.160 --> 00:05:26.320
ciphers and it was quite possibly the first&nbsp;
place where Turing encountered those ideas.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.520
Eight years later he would be working at&nbsp;
the British code breaking organization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:37.840
and helping to develop solutions to&nbsp;
decrypting enigma machine messages.

00:05:37.840 --> 00:05:41.600
Now some of these solutions&nbsp;
that he was helping to develop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.600 --> 00:05:44.960
were first worked out by Polish code breakers.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:49.680
The chapter starts by giving some&nbsp;
definitions of a cryptograph and a cipher.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:55.280
It says here that a cryptograph may&nbsp;
be defined as a manner of writing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.280 --> 00:05:59.600
in which the letters or symbols&nbsp;
employed are used in their normal sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:06.000
but are so arranged that the communication&nbsp;
is intelligible only to those possessing the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.840
key. A simple example is a communication&nbsp;
in which every word is spelt backwards.

00:06:12.400 --> 00:06:20.160
A cipher may be defined as a manner of writing by&nbsp;
characters arbitrarily invented or by an arbitrary&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:25.760
use of letters words or characters in&nbsp;
any other than their ordinary sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.320 --> 00:06:31.840
intelligible only to those possessing&nbsp;
the key. A simple example is when each&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:37.040
letter is replaced by the one that immediately&nbsp;
follows it in the natural order of the alphabet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.760 --> 00:06:43.760
a being replaced by b, b by&nbsp;
c and so on finally z by a.

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:51.280
In a communication cipher symbols may be arranged&nbsp;
cryptographically and thus further hinder a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.280 --> 00:06:57.520
reading of the message so the message above as a&nbsp;
cryptographic cipher would be this quite confusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:03.520
message down here, but of course these messages&nbsp;
get much more complicated than these examples.

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:08.080
Later in the chapter the author goes&nbsp;
on to talk about cipher machines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:08.720 --> 00:07:15.280
he says that there's a risk of some instrument&nbsp;
when set falling into unauthorized hands&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.280 --> 00:07:20.720
and this must be taken into account. Since&nbsp;
equally good ciphers can be constructed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:26.400
without the use of a mechanical device I do&nbsp;
not think their employment can be recommended.

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.160
Now after this was written cipher machines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.160 --> 00:07:36.640
would go on to become the norm and Alan Turing&nbsp;
who read this book would go on to attack the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.640 --> 00:07:42.720
enigma machine which is an example of one,&nbsp;
so perhaps not everything in this book is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:48.000
totally up to date but both of these books&nbsp;
i've mentioned so far are now in the public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.000 --> 00:07:53.360
domain so you can read them yourself for free and&nbsp;
there'll be links in the description to do that.

00:07:54.160 --> 00:08:00.000
Of course there are plenty of other books and&nbsp;
people who inspired Alan Turing throughout his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:06.960
life and in his childhood and we can see another&nbsp;
resource here which is the complete list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:12.720
library books that Alan borrowed while he was a&nbsp;
student at the Sherborne school. As mentioned in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:12.720 --> 00:08:19.440
my previous video he read a lot of books about&nbsp;
math, science, physics and astronomy and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:25.040
so many fiction books and his english teacher&nbsp;
tried to encourage him to read more of those.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:29.920
He did read the Alice in Wonderland books which&nbsp;
did have a bit of a mathematical bend to them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:35.840
but I find looking at this list of books that&nbsp;
he read as a student to be pretty interesting.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:40.800
It would seem to me that even when he was&nbsp;
young he knew what he was interested in and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.800 --> 00:08:45.760
he pursued those things he was interested&nbsp;
in and eventually found success in them.

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:50.000
Thanks as always to my Patreon supporters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:54.880
and a special shout out to today's&nbsp;
Patreon cat of the day Chito.&nbsp;

00:08:56.160 --> 00:09:05.840
Thanks for watching and I&nbsp;
hope to see you next time

